  <revision rev="5" vrev="2">
    <srcmd5>e0d60723bab57a3c51878e49e153e75c</srcmd5>
    <version>0.26.0</version>
    <time>1786303989</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Add a cpu multibuild flavour with vLLM's compiled CPU kernels

python-vllm keeps building exactly as before as the default flavour (noarch,
VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=empty, inference through torch's native CPU operators). This
adds a second flavour, python-vllm-cpu, built with VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=cpu so
vLLM's own optimised C++ CPU kernels are available.

The two install the same importable vllm module, so python-vllm-cpu Provides and
Conflicts python-vllm -- exactly one is installable.

upstream's cmake/cpu_extension.cmake builds oneDNN from source at an exact pin
and, on aarch64, the Arm Compute Library as oneDNN's backend, both fetched from
git at configure time. That cannot work in an offline build root, so both are
shipped as Sources and wired up through the FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_ONEDNN and
ACL_ROOT_DIR environment overrides upstream already honours. The two oneDNN pins
are upstream's own and genuinely differ per arch.

Verified on x86_64 and aarch64.</comment>
    <requestid>1369851</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="6" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>0d400994c87037724866a29b0d7b1795</srcmd5>
